  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 253 AM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nThe line of mid to upper level cloud cover is pushing through \neastern Minnesota and western Wisconsin this morning and has led to \nthe emergence of some patchy fog in a few spots mainly in central MN \nthis morning. Temperatures are running close to dew points in the \nlow 30s, with another freeze for pretty much everyone except for \nthose in the urban core of the Twin Cities. Quiet weather is \nexpected once the morning fog passes with mostly clear to partly \ncloudy skies as a mid level shortwave and weak surface low move \neastwards across central Canada giving us just enough lift to \nproduce some high clouds. South to southwest winds in the lower \nlevels will lead to some weak WAA and high temperatures in the low \nto mid 50s as winds reach around 10mph during peak heating in the \nearly afternoon. The main thing this warm front will do is keep our \novernight lows warmer tomorrow night as further cloud cover is \nexpected to build between the leading warm nose and the weak cold \nfront behind it with said cold front reaching central MN by the \nearly morning hours and through southern Minnesota by sunrise. \nTemperatures will end up similar tomorrow compared to today, with \novernight lows dropping back into the 30s for Monday morning behind \nthe cold front.\n\nA better punch of cooler air looks to hold off until the middle of \nthe week with 850mb temperatures dropping below freezing on \nWednesday as north-northwesterly flow is evident aloft. The leading \nedge of an upper level ridge will be entering the lee-side Rockies \naround this time with a relatively quick rebound in temperatures \ntowards next weekend as the ridge moves through. Highs in the 40s by \nWednesday will return to the 50s to end the week with even warmer \ntemperatures teased within the guidance beyond 7 days as the ridge \nsolidifies over the region. Alongside this ridge, a lack of \nprecipitation is expected due to continued dry air and subsidence \nfrom the ridge suppressing any omega that may try to form. Overall \nthis quiet and above normal weather pattern looks to continue into \nthe middle of the month, and we will have to see if it continues to \nhold on towards the later parts of the month as we approach the \nThanksgiving holiday.",
